# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Datasets -- NEVER committed.
#
# Both dataset versions are publicly archived (see README.md) and are large
# binary blobs that git handles badly. They are inputs to this study, not
# products of it, so they are reproduced by download instruction rather than
# stored here. Excluding the whole directory also covers the metadata CSV,
# which ships inside the dataset archives.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `data/*` rather than `data/`: git cannot re-include anything beneath an
# excluded DIRECTORY, and data/kaggle/ below needs exactly that. Excluding the
# children instead keeps every dataset out while leaving one door openable.
data/*

# The corrected-split tree built by scripts/build_corrected_dataset.py. Already
# covered by `data/*` above; named explicitly because it is GENERATED rather than
# downloaded, and a reader checking "is my rebuilt dataset excluded?" should be
# able to find the answer by searching for it. Rebuild it with that script from
# runs/20260803_corrected_split_02/split_manifest.csv rather than committing
# 146 MB of images that are byte-identical to v2's.
data/ElectroCom-61_corrected/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kaggle training results.
#
# The SMALL TEXT is committed -- it is the evidence behind every number in the
# paper: the ten results_*.json, the per-class and training-curve CSVs, and the
# args.yaml recording each run's hyperparameters. Together ~150 KB.
#
# Everything large stays out. This repository is public, and the artifact zips
# plus their plots and batch mosaics are ~240 MB of binaries that no result
# depends on: 194 .jpg (144 MB), 190 .png (96 MB), 2 .html Kaggle exports
# (1 MB), and 4 inner .json epoch dumps (3.9 MB, distinct from and far larger
# than the top-level results files).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!data/kaggle/
data/kaggle/*.zip
data/kaggle/artifacts/**/*.png
data/kaggle/artifacts/**/*.jpg
data/kaggle/artifacts/**/*.jpeg
data/kaggle/artifacts/**/*.html
data/kaggle/artifacts/**/*.json
data/kaggle/artifacts/**/*.pt
data/kaggle/artifacts/**/*.pth
data/kaggle/artifacts/**/*.onnx

# The source bundle itself, wherever it is dropped in the tree.
collected_bundle.zip

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The master results table built by scripts/collect_results.py. Derived, but
# small text and the backbone of every accuracy figure in the paper, so it is
# committed and diffable rather than regenerated silently.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!data/master_results.csv

# Per-architecture latency means and pair gaps, built by
# scripts/latency_by_arch.py. Same rationale: small text, and it carries the
# repeatability statement the latency claims rest on.
!data/latency_by_arch.csv

# Training-configuration provenance. Scaffolded from the committed args.yaml
# by scripts/scaffold_config_provenance.py, then hand-filled: the source and
# citation columns are a citation rather than a measurement and exist nowhere
# else in the repository. It must be versioned, and it must never be
# regenerated over -- the scaffold script refuses to overwrite it.
!data/config_provenance.csv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the prior papers report for layers and parameters, typed by hand from
# their PDFs. Committed for the same reason as config_provenance.csv above: it
# is a reading of the literature, not a measurement, so NO SCRIPT CAN
# REGENERATE IT. If it is lost it has to be re-transcribed from the sources.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!data/published_complexity.csv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the prior papers report for ACCURACY. Same discipline as
# published_complexity.csv above and same warning: hand-transcribed from the
# PDFs, NO SCRIPT CAN REGENERATE IT, and every row names the table it came from
# so any value can be re-checked against its source in one step. It is in long
# form rather than wide because the three sources report different quantities:
# the dataset paper five per model, the benchmark studies one.
#
# WHY THE 95.9% ROW IS SAFE TO ATTRIBUTE, WHEN README.md HEDGES THE SAME FIGURE.
# These are two different objects and only one of them is hedged.
#   - THE PAPER. The dataset paper states in its own text, as well as in
#     Table 3, that 95.9% is YOLOv9s at mAP@50. This file records what the
#     paper reports, which is a quotation and needs no hedge.
#   - THE NOTEBOOK. README.md's "Reference material" section is an analysis of
#     reference/electrocom61-yolov9.ipynb, the archived Kaggle notebook. What it
#     records is that the notebook trains TWO models (gelan-c, yolov9-e) without
#     tying either to a result, and that the per-class dict whose mean is 0.9593
#     is unlabelled as to whether it is mAP@50, mAP@50-95, precision or recall.
#     That is a claim about what the NOTEBOOK evidences, not about what the
#     PAPER attributes.
# Section 2.1's deliberately metric-free wording ("a headline figure of 95.9%")
# was adopted because of the second object and still stands for it. Section 1's
# concrete instance cites the first, via this file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!data/published_accuracy.csv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the complexity-proxy papers actually claim. Same discipline again: read
# by hand from their full texts, NO SCRIPT CAN REGENERATE IT.
#
# It exists because of a real citation error. 2.4 and 7.4 both said "parameter
# counts and FLOPs both correlate poorly with measured on-device latency" and
# cited MobileOne and FasterNet together. Both halves were wrong. FasterNet says
# nothing about parameter counts and reports no correlation coefficients at all;
# MobileOne reports an ASYMMETRY -- latency weakly correlated with parameter
# count and moderately with FLOPs -- which "both poorly" flattened, and which
# our own 6.4 independently finds.
#
# The third row records a negative: that FasterNet reports no coefficients. That
# is the fact whose absence allowed the error, so it is written down rather than
# left to be rediscovered.
#
# THE source_location COLUMN IS INCOMPLETE for the two MobileOne rows. The
# coefficients came from the author's reading of the full text and the section
# or table they sit in was not recorded. Fill it before submission; until then
# these two values cannot be re-checked in one step, which is the whole purpose
# of the column.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!data/published_proxy_claims.csv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Text extractions of the built PDF, produced with miktex-pdftotext for upload.
# Derived from paper/main.pdf, which is itself ignored, so committing them would
# version a copy of a build product and let it drift from the PDF silently.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
paper/main-full.txt
paper/main-part*.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backup taken by the injection tests for scripts/check_figures_readme.py: the
# checker reads a fixed path, so testing it means mutating figures/README.md in
# place and restoring it afterwards. The restore is verified byte-for-byte; the
# backup is working state and is ignored so a failed run cannot commit one.
# It cannot be deleted from here -- scripts/deny_guard.py denies deletion --
# so remove it by hand.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
figures/README.md.injectbak

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LaTeX build output. The PDF is regenerated by scripts/build_paper.ps1 from
# sources that are all tracked; the auxiliary files are intermediate state and
# change on every run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
paper/main.pdf
paper/*.aux
paper/*.log
paper/*.bbl
paper/*.blg
paper/*.out
paper/*.toc
paper/*.fls
paper/*.fdb_latexmk
paper/*.synctex.gz

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python bytecode -- regenerated on every run, machine-specific.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-machine tool permissions. Not part of the study and not portable to
# another checkout. CLAUDE.md (the actual project rules) IS committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.claude/settings.local.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OS / editor droppings.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Split fragments from an in-place rebuild of references.bib. These are working
# files, not artifacts: the assistant wrote them to reassemble the bibliography
# and cannot delete them, because file deletion is denied by
# scripts/deny_guard.py. Ignored so they never reach a commit. Safe to delete
# by hand.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
paper/references.bib.head
paper/references.bib.mid
paper/references.bib.tail
paper/main.bbl.bak
